### `assets/kit/quality.js`
- Origin: `src/player/quality.js` from **world-execute-web** (same revision, copyright and licence as above)
- Changes:
  1. the cost table became optional — with no table every guard and the learning loop still run, so a
     film that was never calibrated still adapts (upstream required the table its own film ships);
  2. `sizeFor`/`megapixels` take the film's design aspect instead of a hard-coded 16:9;
  3. the reference player's motion-blur and `mbCap` coupling is not carried into this file;
  4. names, argument order, the ladder, the budgets and every threshold are kept as published;
  5. `calibrate()` rejects a two-factor fit the samples cannot support (it tested only for an exactly
     singular system, which mis-fits films whose frames cost a couple of milliseconds);
  6. the file also carries `midTime`, `shotMs` and `pickCalibration`, derived from upstream
     `src/player/pick.js` at the same revision, reading this kit's `shot.join` and an optional design
     aspect instead of upstream's `shot.transitionIn` and fixed 16:9.
- The resolution ladder, the up/down rules and the weighted least-squares fit are the upstream work.

### `assets/kit/transport.js`
- Origin: `src/player/player.js` from **world-execute-web** (same revision, copyright and licence as above)
- Changes:
  1. drives this kit's engine (a flat shot list, `engine.draw(t)`) instead of the reference engine's
     chapters, motion blur and output-frame-rate coupling;
  2. the song is fetched and decoded from a file the project already holds — the reference player's
     remote key service is not part of this kit and nothing here talks to it;
  3. added `setRate`: any rate other than 1 plays silently off a virtual clock;
  4. calibration runs only when the film ships a measured cost table (`scripts/calibrate.mjs`), and
     samples the chosen shots at two heights rather than one;
  5. the storage prefix is per-film, and `startOptions` gained `?rate=`.
- The audio-led clock, the vsync pacing, the context-loss recovery and the frame-time feedback loop
  are the upstream design.

## Deliberately not taken

- `src/player/vault.js` — the client half of the upstream project's encrypted-audio service (ECDH to
  their key server, AES-GCM parts of their song). It cannot run without their server, and using it
  against them would be circumventing their access control, which the AGPL does not grant. This kit
  plays a local audio file instead.
- The numbers in `src/player/cost.json.js` — a per-shot frame-cost table measured on the upstream
  author's own machine (`Apple M5 · Chrome/154.0.8037.95`) for their own film. Only the *mechanism*
  is reproduced here, fed by `scripts/calibrate.mjs` measuring each project on its own.

## Reference only (no code taken)

Everything not listed above is original work. The engine's architecture — shots as data, absolute
song time, joins declared on the incoming shot, text layers composited before and after grading,
analysis-driven key functions — was studied in the same upstream project without copying its code.
`src/player/ui.js`, `src/player/config.js`, `src/player/subs.js` and `src/subs/*` are still in that
category; where they are ported later they will get entries here first.
A read-only checkout for reference may be placed at `third_party/world-execute-web/`; it is not
committed and is not part of this work's source.

